","callId":"call_aeee27c0987a42378c5e3a20"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_aeee27c0987a42378c5e3a20","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"definition\":{\"id\":\"cssci-paper\",\"name\":\"CSSCI 期刊论文\",\"description\":\"面向 CSSCI 来源期刊的中文论文写作：真实中文文献 + 学科风格 + 期刊适配。\",\"instructions\":\"中文文献检索优先 ncpssd_search（默认核心期刊），核心标记必须来自来源白名单。\\n写作遵循学科风格（马理论：经典文本与历史/现实逻辑；社会学：机制、结构、行动者与经验验证；政治学：制度、权力与治理；等等），不得全部写成同一种通用学术语言。\\n禁止套话堆砌与机械\\\"首先其次再次\\\"；每章承担明确论证任务，段落为论证链服务。\\n凡引用文献必须有真实来源并已在项目保存；无法核验的条目不得进入正式成果。\",\"requiredTools\":[\"literature_search\",\"ncpssd_search\",\"literature_save\"],\"goalTemplate\":\"完成面向 CSSCI 的《{{topic}}》论文：真实中文文献 ≥ N 篇 + 学科化写作 + 可投稿草稿。\",\"evidencePolicy\":\"require-evidence-for-citations\",\"outputContract\":\"cssci paper artifact（markdown/docx）\"}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"91e195f4-5738-4581-8693-dd80d48c1554"}},"failed":false},{"name":"scenario_get","args":"{\"id\":\"literature-review\"}","result":{"turn":1,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_f2dc650521c54bdca8b3c9f4"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_f2dc650521c54bdca8b3c9f4","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"definition\":{\"id\":\"literature-review\",\"name\":\"文献综述\",\"description\":\"围绕研究主题系统检索中英文文献，形成知识结构化综述（研究传统/争议/缺口/趋势）。\",\"instructions\":\"先明确研究主题的核心概念与同义词，形成多通道检索计划（核心概念/理论/机制/方法/学者/经典/近期/反向观点）。\\n中文文献用 ncpssd_search（默认核心期刊），英文用 literature_search（Crossref/OpenAlex 真实来源）。必须实际调用工具获取文献，禁止凭记忆列出任何文献。\\n对每篇纳入文献区分：研究问题、理论视角、方法与数据、核心结论。不同证据等级不得混成统一陈述。\\n综合时回答：该领域现在知道什么？还不知道什么？为什么不知道（事实/理论/机制/方法/情境缺口）？接下来应该研究什么？\\n综述正文引用的每篇文献必须已在项目中保存（literature_save），不得出现无来源条目。\",\"requiredTools\":[\"literature_search\",\"literature_save\",\"ncpssd_search\"],\"goalTemplate\":\"完成《{{topic}}》文献综述：真实文献 ≥ N 篇，形成研究传统/争议/缺口结构，产出可继续使用的综述文件。\",\"evidencePolicy\":\"require-evidence-for-citations\",\"outputContract\":\"literature-review artifact（markdown，含分主题知识结构与缺口判定）\"}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"b66d6401-11a9-4cee-929a-b0af13d59c7c"}},"failed":false},{"name":"scenario_get","args":"{\"id\":\"theoretical-paper\"}","result":{"turn":1,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_c1b1e456e45d4fadaa4a54c2"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_c1b1e456e45d4fadaa4a54c2","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"definition\":{\"id\":\"theoretical-paper\",\"name\":\"理论论文\",\"description\":\"构建有理论依据的机制框架：X → 机制 → Y，并明确条件与边界。\",\"instructions\":\"核心概念必须有正式理论来源；使用含义与学界一般定义不一致时要明确指出，不得顺着错误概念续写。\\n理论分析不停留在 X 影响 Y：追问为什么/通过什么过程/在什么条件下/对哪些主体/什么时间尺度/哪些中介与边界。\\n形成机制链（X → 机制 A → 机制 B → Y，受条件 C/D 与制度环境 E 影响），遵循所属学科的概念体系与理论传统。\\n区分理论命题与已验证结论：未经验证的机制判断必须标注为待检验命题。\\n主动反向检查：反例、替代机制、概念重叠、上位/下位概念混淆、分析层级错位。\",\"requiredTools\":[\"literature_search\"],\"goalTemplate\":\"完成《{{topic}}》理论论文：机制框架 + 概念澄清 + 反向检查 + 可继续发展的理论命题。\",\"evidencePolicy\":\"require-evidence-for-citations\",\"outputContract\":\"theoretical paper artifact（markdown，含机制图与命题清单）\"}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"f557c314-0602-4b6a-8b25-6c7228e586c3"}},"failed":false},{"name":"scenario_get","args":"{\"id\":\"empirical-paper\"}","result":{"turn":1,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_6794577a472948d49fb41ffa"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_6794577a472948d49fb41ffa","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"definition\":{\"id\":\"empirical-paper\",\"name\":\"实证论文\",\"description\":\"从研究问题推出研究设计与数据需求，真实执行分析并形成论证完整的实证论文。\",\"instructions\":\"研究设计必须从研究问题反推：问题 → 需要什么证据 → 需要什么数据 → 研究设计 → 方法。不得因\\\"会某方法\\\"而设计问题。\\n数据分析必须真实执行：读取数据 → 检查变量 → 清洗 → 按方案执行代码 → 稳健性检查。图表中的数字必须来自真实计算并可追溯代码。\\n结果与预期相反时如实报告，禁止为论文故事修改结果。\\n对核心命题执行反向检查：替代解释、因果倒置、第三变量、已有文献是否已解释。\\n论文按论证链组织：研究问题 → 已有研究及不足 → 理论判断 → 分析框架 → 证据 → 分析 → 结果 → 理论解释 → 结论。\",\"requiredTools\":[\"literature_search\",\"literature_save\"],\"goalTemplate\":\"完成《{{topic}}》实证论文：真实数据分析（可追溯代码）+ 完整论证链 + 可投稿草稿。\",\"evidencePolicy\":\"require-evidence-for-citations\",\"outputContract\":\"empirical paper artifact（markdown/docx，含数据、代码与图表引用）\"}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"e23182f4-dde7-4749-8e74-4ef5901dc90f"}},"failed":false},{"name":"scenario_activate","args":"{\"id\":\"theoretical-paper\"}","result":{"turn":1,"step":4,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_d7bb676305894db9ace4eeba"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_d7bb676305894db9ace4eeba","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"ok\":true,\"activeId\":\"theoretical-paper\",\"missingTools\":[],\"validationState\":\"validated\",\"goal\":{\"id\":\"goal-d887335f-0ef5-41e1-94ae-8b85bd760402\",\"revision\":1,\"objective\":\"完成《平台经济中的算法管理与劳动者自主性》理论论文：机制框架 + 概念澄清 + 反向检查 + 可继续发展的理论命题。\",\"phase\":\"active\",\"maxGoalRounds\":256,\"roundsStarted\":0,\"createdAt\":1789402067501,\"updatedAt\":1789402067501,\"activation\":\"armed\"}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"7cde70d1-b53a-4db0-a2c3-279e42e8f330"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"query\":\"algorithmic management platform labor workers\",\"limit\":20,\"providers\":\"crossref,openalex\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":1,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_0bf78c08d7914cada86767a4"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_0bf78c08d7914cada86767a4","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":20,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1080/0023656x.2025.2600998\",\"title\":\"Flickering agency: How algorithmic management shapes workers’ agency in the platform economy in India?\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Digvijay Singh\"},{\"name\":\"Anindya Jayanta Mishra\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Labor History\",\"doi\":\"10.1080/0023656x.2025.2600998\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1080/0023656x.2025.2600998\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5614/sostek.itbj.2022.21.2.5\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Exploitation: Understanding Labor Process and Control among RideHailing Platform Workers\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"M. Falikul Isbah\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Jurnal Sosioteknologi\",\"abstract\":\"This study analyzes the labor process of ride-hailing workers and the platformcompany’s control over them. This research employs Labour Process Theory (LPT)to examine the labor process and control critically. Based on in-depth interviewswith motorcycle drivers working for Go-Jek and Grab between June 2020 and June2021 in Yogyakarta, Kediri, and Jakarta, this research found that labor or workersare transformed into a commodity where job supply and demand are confounded andmediated by digital platforms. Algorithmic management, utilized in this industry, playsa significant role in this digital industry, including its use as a point of production and asa mechanism of control and monitoring in the workplace. The use of feedback systems,ratings, and platform ratings as a consequence of emotional work is used by managersto help monitor their employees. Furthermore, this study reveals that the form of controlconducted by the platform represents an unequal power relation, which producesdissatisfaction and conflicts. We argue that the labor process in this job resembles whatwe coined “algorithmic exploitation,” which means that the platform companies aredeliberately using technology and obscuring the control process over the operations ofthe workers to instill the high standards of service, while the workers are trapped in avery weak employment status as “partners.”\",\"doi\":\"10.5614/sostek.itbj.2022.21.2.5\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5614/sostek.itbj.2022.21.2.5\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":6},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4324/9781003674931-9\",\"title\":\"Regulations on Algorithmic Management\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Zhenxing Ke\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Legal Protections of Platform Workers in China\",\"doi\":\"10.4324/9781003674931-9\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003674931-9\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.22381/pihrm6120187\",\"title\":\"THE ECONOMICS OF THE ONLINE GIG ECONOMY: ALGORITHMIC HIRING PRACTICES, DIGITAL LABOR-MARKET INTERMEDIATION, AND RIGHTS FOR PLATFORM WORKERS\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2018,\"journal\":\"Psychosociological Issues in Human Resource Management\",\"doi\":\"10.22381/pihrm6120187\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.22381/pihrm6120187\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":4},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.5161650\",\"title\":\"House of gigs. Domestic workers, algorithmic management and the Platform Work Directive\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Antonio Aloisi\"},{\"name\":\"Nastazja Potocka-Sionek\"}],\"year\":2025,\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.5161650\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5161650\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.36676/jams.v3.i2.99\",\"title\":\"The Gig Economy and Its Workers: Platform Labor, Algorithmic Control, and the Paradox of Flexible Precarity\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Mr. Himanshu Gaur\"},{\"name\":\"Dr. Geeta Yadav\"},{\"name\":\"Dr. Himani Kargeti\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"Journal of Advanced Management Studies\",\"abstract\":\"The gig economy has fundamentally restructured traditional employment relationships by introducing platform-mediated, algorithmically managed, and temporally fragmented work arrangements. Yet the theoretical mechanisms through which these arrangements simultaneously enable worker flexibility and systematically produce precarity remain contested across disciplinary boundaries. This narrative theoretical review synthesizes the multidisciplinary literature on gig workers, organizing the research conversation around a central theoretical puzzle: how do platform-mediated work arrangements simultaneously promise enhanced worker autonomy while structurally generating economic instability, diminished bargaining power, and constrained agency? Drawing on a literature set spanning 2016–2026, the review identifies five interconnected thematic tensions that structure the field: (1) definitional and taxonomic ambiguity in conceptualizing gig work and its boundaries; (2) the flexibility–precarity paradox as a structural feature rather than an empirical anomaly; (3) algorithmic management as a novel mechanism of control that reconfigures traditional employer–employee power asymmetries; (4) worker well-being and the psychosocial costs of non-standard employment across heterogeneous worker populations; and (5) worker agency, resistance, and emergent forms of collective organization under conditions of structural atomization. Through cross-theme integration, the review reveals that platform governance operates as the pivotal mediating mechanism connecting macro-institutional environments, organizational control structures, and micro-level worker experiences. A multi-level conceptual framework is proposed that situates these tensions within an integrated model of downward causation and upward feedback.\",\"doi\":\"10.36676/jams.v3.i2.99\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.36676/jams.v3.i2.99\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.3390/admsci16090437\",\"title\":\"Beyond Platform Work: Algorithmic Management in Platform Labor and Traditional Organizations—The Hungarian Experience\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Tamás Zelles\"},{\"name\":\"József Pap\"},{\"name\":\"Csaba Makó\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"Administrative Sciences\",\"abstract\":\"Algorithmic management (AM) is spreading rapidly in platform work and traditional employment, yet empirical research remains platform-centric and skewed towards Western Europe, North America and China, leaving Central and Eastern Europe, especially Hungary, largely absent. This paper reports qualitative fieldwork on AM in two contrasting Hungarian firms, Data Analytics, a knowledge-intensive business services company, and ConLog, a contract logistics provider. Fieldwork in the EU-funded INCODING project (January 2022–July 2023) combined 15 semi-structured interviews, twelve internal and three external, with participant observation and documentary analysis. A sample limitation applies to one of the two firms: the Data Analytics evidence rests on only four internal interviews (two employees and two managers), so the findings for that case are indicative and exploratory rather than firm-level conclusions. A deliberately asymmetric comparison sets these findings against Hungarian platform labor, using secondary evidence on Uber, Wolt and Bolt from the CrowdWork21 project (2019–2021). Results show heterogeneity. In traditional firms, algorithmic systems sit within managerial hierarchies and produce hybrid human-algorithmic control. The older platform evidence documents real-time monitoring, opaque decision rules and market-mediated feedback that tighten control and erode autonomy. The contrast is a proposition for testing, not a measured difference. Workers in both settings adapt opportunistically, accommodating or partially resisting control. Hungary’s weak industrial relations institutions remain theoretically instructive despite limited generalizability. The contribution lies in the platform versus non-platform comparison, the institutional-bypassing argument and the design authority continuum.\",\"doi\":\"10.3390/admsci16090437\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.3390/admsci16090437\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.33767/osf.io/7ejqn\",\"title\":\"Platform scams: Brazilian workers’ experiences of dishonest and uncertain algorithmic management\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Rafael Grohmann\"},{\"name\":\"Gabriel Pereira\"},{\"name\":\"Abel Guerra\"},{\"name\":\"Ludmila Costhek Abílio\"},{\"name\":\"Bruno Moreschi\"},{\"name\":\"Amanda Jurno\"}],\"year\":2021,\"abstract\":\"This article discusses how Brazilian platform workers experience and respond to platform scams through three case studies. Drawing from digital ethnographic research, vlogs/interviews of workers, and literature review, we argue for a conceptualization of “platform scam” that focuses on multiple forms of platform dishonesty and uncertainty. We characterize scam as a structuring element of the algorithmic management enacted by platform labor. The first case engages with when platforms scam workers by discussing Uber drivers’ experiences with the illusive surge pricing. The second case discusses when workers (have to) scam platforms by focusing on Amazon Mechanical Turk microworkers’ experiences with faking their identities. The third case presents when platforms lead workers to scam third parties, by engaging with how Brazilian click farm platforms’ workers use bots/fake accounts to engage with social media. Our focus on “platform scams” thus highlights the particular dimensions of faking, fraud, and deception operating in platform labor. This notion of platform scam expands and complexifies the understanding of scam within platform labor studies. Departing from workers' experiences, we engage with the asymmetries and unequal power relations present in the algorithmic management of labor.\",\"doi\":\"10.33767/osf.io/7ejqn\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.33767/osf.io/7ejqn\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4324/9781003674931-6\",\"title\":\"Exploring the New Labor Classification of Platform Workers\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Zhenxing Ke\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Legal Protections of Platform Workers in China\",\"doi\":\"10.4324/9781003674931-6\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003674931-6\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4324/9781003674931-5\",\"title\":\"Early Practice of Labor Classification of Platform Workers\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Zhenxing Ke\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Legal Protections of Platform Workers in China\",\"doi\":\"10.4324/9781003674931-5\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003674931-5\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.63208/21014-342\",\"title\":\"Platform Capitalism and the Reorganization of Labor: Algorithmic Management and Market Concentration\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Risfanda Nurhudiana\"},{\"name\":\"Rosita Permatahati\"}],\"year\":2023,\"journal\":\"Integra Business Review\",\"abstract\":\"The rapid expansion of digital platform economies has transformed contemporary economic systems by reshaping labor participation, market coordination, and digitally mediated economic activities. Platform-based systems such as gig economy applications, online labor platforms, and digital marketplaces increasingly function as central infrastructures within modern digital economies. The findings indicate that platform economies contribute positively to economic transformation by increasing market accessibility, labor flexibility, digital connectivity, and participation within online economic ecosystems. However, the review also reveals significant governance and socio-economic challenges associated with platform-based systems, including labor precarity, algorithmic management, platform dependency, market concentration, and unequal economic participation. In addition, governance systems frequently struggle to adapt to rapidly evolving digital platform environments characterized by cross-border operations and data-driven coordination mechanisms. The study concludes that sustainable platform economies require adaptive governance frameworks capable of balancing technological innovation, labor protection, market fairness, and inclusive economic participation within digitally mediated economic systems.\",\"doi\":\"10.63208/21014-342\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.63208/21014-342\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.4606803\",\"title\":\"Exercising Workers Rights in Algorithmic Management Systems: Lessons Learned from the Glovo-Foodinho Digital Labour Platform Case\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Claudio Agosti\"},{\"name\":\"Joanna Bronowicka\"},{\"name\":\"Alessandro Polidoro\"},{\"name\":\"Gaetano Priori\"}],\"year\":2023,\"abstract\":\"With the Platform Work Directive still under negotiation and the ETUC calling for an EU Directive on Algorithmic Systems at Work, groundbreaking research into the Spanish-Italian Glovo-Foodinho firm, a digital labour platform, has opened up an alternative path for workers wanting to scrutinize application and access their rights in digitalised workplaces, governed by algorithmic management. This involves closed cooperation between trade unions, reverse engineering and Data protection authorities (DPA).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;As illustrated in this report, the current GDPR legislative framework has proven to be both robust and flexible. The report also reveals the details of the collaboration between ETUI and a technical team of experts led by Claudio Agosti into the so-called ’black box’. It details how the algorithmic management systems used by Foodinho, an Italian subsidiary of the Spanish food delivery company Glovo, works to control its workforce, namely:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Tracking riders’ location outside working hours.&lt;br&gt;Sharing the location (both outside and during working hours), along with other personal data, with undisclosed third parties not mentioned in the app’s documentation.&lt;br&gt;Generating a score associated with the rider’s personal profile, a value inconsistent with the documented ‘Excellence Score’ provided by Glovo-Foodinho: a hidden score.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Background&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;In 2019, algorithm analyst, Claudio Agosti, started to work with the ETUI Foresight Unit on research and training courses aimed at understanding the ‘technicalities’ behind Artificial Intelligence. Five years later, the ETUI is releasing a technical report which meticulously outlines the techniques used by researchers to observe the internal logic of the app used by riders in Italy and documents its actual behavior in terms of harvesting their personal data.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The technical analysis was made in 2021-2022-2023, after Italian DPA, responsible for enforcing the EU’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) fined the company 2,600,000 euros in 2021 for failing to protect the digital rights of its workers. Glovo appealed. They did not pay the fine and as released in this report, they have continued the same unregular practices. Finally, these new evidences have been reported to the Italian DPA.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;New challenges for workers’ rights&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;According to the authors of this ETUI report, collecting evidence about algorithmic management is critical for workers seeking to enforce their rights by filing complaints with national DPAs or the competent courts. The availability of technical expertise can also help improve workers’ negotiating positions in a collective bargaining context. “Ultimately, we believe that, by helping workers understand the apps that control them, we can give them the key to unlocking their rights, thus building a fairer future of work for all.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.4606803\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4606803\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":6},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.4703474\",\"title\":\"A Caring Approach to Algorithmic Management: A Model for Enhancing Platform Workers' Motivation and Optimal Functioning\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Dimitris Giamos\"},{\"name\":\"Antoine Bujold\"},{\"name\":\"Olivier Doucet\"},{\"name\":\"Xavier Parent-Rocheleau\"}],\"year\":2024,\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.4703474\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4703474\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.7311598\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Wage Opacity and Platform Labor Monopsony: A Structured Narrative Review\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Ojas Chawla\"}],\"year\":2026,\"abstract\":\"Digital labor platforms increasingly use algorithmic systems to determine worker assignments, compensation, incentives, and access to work. This structured narrative review examines whether algorithmic wage opacity can function as an information-based mechanism that generates or amplifies labor-market monopsony, and whether conventional welfare and antitrust analyses adequately capture that mechanism. The review synthesizes evidence from labor economics, platform-work research, information economics, behavioral economics, and antitrust scholarship. The broader literature provides strong evidence of employer wage-setting power, including low labor-supply elasticities and substantial wage markdowns, although estimates vary across settings and identification strategies. Platform-specific evidence documents individualized and dynamically changing compensation, information asymmetries, reduced predictability, and distributional effects, but direct causal estimates isolating the marginal effect of opacity on monopsony remain limited. Information-economics models provide a plausible bridge: opacity can increase effective search and information costs, alter worker beliefs about outside options, and thereby reduce effective responsiveness to platform compensation. Behavioral evidence, however, cautions against assuming that information costs are linear or that greater transparency is uniformly welfare-enhancing. U.S. merger policy already recognizes labor markets as buyer markets; the unresolved issue is whether existing economic and legal tools adequately measure information-mediated and individualized sources of labor-market power. The strongest defensible conclusion is therefore not that algorithmic opacity constitutes a proven new form of monopsony, but that it is a plausible and potentially measurable mechanism through which effective labor-supply elasticity may be reduced. The review identifies a precise empirical gap: estimating the marginal opacity effect while separating it from concentration, search frictions, dynamic pricing, worker heterogeneity, and other sources of power.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.7311598\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7311598\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/amproc.2025.22746abstract\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Control in Non-Platform Organizations – Workers’ Legitimacy Judgments\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Felix Hirsch\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Proceedings\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/amproc.2025.22746abstract\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/amproc.2025.22746abstract\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1108/cms-08-2023-0372\",\"title\":\"How do algorithms influence platform workers’ behavior? Relationship between algorithmic autonomy support and platform workers’ stress and incivility\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Ting Deng\"},{\"name\":\"Chunyong Tang\"},{\"name\":\"Ang Zhou\"},{\"name\":\"Yanzhao Lai\"}],\"year\":2024,\"journal\":\"Chinese Management Studies\",\"abstract\":\"Purpose Drawing upon the needs–supplies fit theory, this study aims to examine how the expected and perceived algorithmic autonomy support (AAS) influences platform workers’ work stress and incivility, with a focus on the moderating role of self-direction. Design/methodology/approach Using data collected from 422 platform workers in China through multiple waves, the model is tested through polynomial regression and response surface analysis. Findings The results indicate that a mismatch between expected and perceived AAS is associated with higher levels of incivility among platform workers, and this relationship is mediated by work stress. These findings are particularly salient when self-direction is emphasized. Practical implications This study suggests that a universally high level of AAS may not necessarily reduce workers’ stress and incivility. Instead, it is important for platforms to ensure that their workers’ expectations of autonomy support are met and for workers to be given the space to exercise self-direction. Originality/value Previous studies have highlighted the need to pay attention to workers in mobile and ambiguous environments, and this study adds to this literature by focusing specifically on platform organizations and workplaces. This study provides valuable insights into the relationship between AAS, work stress and platform workers’ incivility.\",\"doi\":\"10.1108/cms-08-2023-0372\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1108/cms-08-2023-0372\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":4},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.6225518\",\"title\":\"The Micro-Dynamics of Algorithmic Discipline: A Mathematical Framework for Platform Labor\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Gu Luoluo\"}],\"year\":2026,\"abstract\":\"This paper developsa self-contained mathematical framework for analyzing labor dynamics under algorithmic management in platform economies, with particular application to the food delivery gig economy. We formalize the micro-foundations of rider-agent behavior through a continuous three-dimensional state space integrating economic position, physiological condition, and psychological disposition, drawing on prospect theory, psychological contract theory, and occupational health science. The framework yields three core analytical results. Theorem 1 (Health Depletion Fixed Point) establishes existence, uniqueness, and local asymptotic stability of the health steady state under general convex damage and concave recovery functions. Theorem 2 (Endogenous Stratification) proves that income inequality, measured by the Gini coefficient, is monotonically increasing over time if and only if the platform's dispatch allocation function is strictly convex-formally identifying algorithmic convexity as the mathematical root of the Matthew Effect. Theorem 3 (Inverted-U Control) demonstrates the existence and uniqueness of an interior optimal control intensity that maximizes aggregate order fulfillment, arising from the tension between compliance gains and attrition losses. Together, these results provide a unified analytical foundation for agent-based simulation, empirical testing, and policy design in the platform labor literature.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.6225518\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6225518\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1177/14614448261472307\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic muteness: How algorithmic management obstructs platform content operators’ resonance with their labor\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Zhenyu Qiu\"},{\"name\":\"Jinfeng Du\"},{\"name\":\"Irina Y. Yu\"},{\"name\":\"Jiankun Gong\"},{\"name\":\"Yu Zuo\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"New Media &amp; Society\",\"abstract\":\"Workers who plan, curate, and promote content fuel platforms’ traffic success, yet frequently experience their labor as leaving no meaningful trace. While critical scholarship links this estrangement to algorithmic management, how it reshapes the quality of workers’ relationship with their own labor remains underexplored. Drawing on Rosa’s Resonance Theory and 27 interviews with platform content operators at China’s leading internet companies, this study reveals how algorithmic management obstructs resonance across content production, distribution, and feedback. Quantification hollows out labor’s normative worth; black-boxing forecloses self-efficacy through the (un)controllability paradox; amplification and acceleration suspend the possibility of self-transformation, even amid measurable success. We conceptualize ‘algorithmic muteness’, which captures a relational state—workers maintain high-intensity, data-mediated contact with their labor, yet this contact rarely yields genuine responsiveness. Algorithmic muteness moves beyond documenting the static, structural consequences of algorithmic management (e.g. alienation, meaninglessness) to tracing the relational process producing them, while critically revealing algorithms’ deep entanglement with platform capital.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/14614448261472307\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/14614448261472307\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4324/9781003674931-11\",\"title\":\"Other Key Themes in the Protection of Platform Workers' Labor Rights\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Zhenxing Ke\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Legal Protections of Platform Workers in China\",\"doi\":\"10.4324/9781003674931-11\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003674931-11\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.6007/ijarbss/v16-i8/28689\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Management and Platform Workers' Ethical Challenges\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Siti Suraya Abd Razak\"},{\"name\":\"Yosuke Uchiyama\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"International Journal of Academic Research in Business and Social Sciences\",\"doi\":\"10.6007/ijarbss/v16-i8/28689\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.6007/ijarbss/v16-i8/28689\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\",\"openalex\"],\"providerFailures\":[\"openalex: Literature provider \\\"openalex\\\" is unavailable: rate_limited(429). 其他来源仍可使用；本次未返回伪结果。\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"2e7e59f6-e35c-418a-a7ae-4a727e7a43db"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"query\":\"platform work autonomy labor process control gig economy\",\"limit\":20,\"providers\":\"openalex,crossref\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":1,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_e209ac5e322d42879bf12b75"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_e209ac5e322d42879bf12b75","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":20,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"title\":\"Toward Convivial Platform Labor\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00009\",\"title\":\"Platform economy puzzles: the need for a multidisciplinary perspective on gig work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Jeroen Meijerink\"},{\"name\":\"Giedo Jansen\"},{\"name\":\"Victoria Daskalova\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00009\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00009\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":5},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"title\":\"Alienation in the Platform Economy\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00013\",\"title\":\"Unpacking platform economy puzzles - economic and social exchanges in platform-mediated gig work\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00013\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00013\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.3383159\",\"title\":\"Regulating the Autonomy of Gig Workers. A Paternalizing Look into the Consent-Based Platform Work Economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Marta Santos Silva\"},{\"name\":\"Mijke Houwerzijl\"}],\"year\":2019,\"journal\":\"SSRN Electronic Journal\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.3383159\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3383159\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.17760/d20487636\",\"title\":\"Labor control and the experience of work in the platform economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Christopher Tirrell\"}],\"year\":null,\"doi\":\"10.17760/d20487636\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.17760/d20487636\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":3},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4324/9781003161875-34\",\"title\":\"Platform economy and gig work in South Korea\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Ji-Hyeon Kim\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"The Routledge Handbook of the Gig Economy\",\"doi\":\"10.4324/9781003161875-34\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003161875-34\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00011\",\"title\":\"The past, present and future of gig work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Jim Stanford\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00011\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00011\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":3},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00008\",\"title\":\"Setting the stage - platform-mediated gig work in context\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00008\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00008\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1177/0950017018785616\",\"title\":\"Good Gig, Bad Gig: Autonomy and Algorithmic Control in the Global Gig Economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Alex J Wood\"},{\"name\":\"Mark Graham\"},{\"name\":\"Vili Lehdonvirta\"},{\"name\":\"Isis Hjorth\"}],\"year\":2018,\"journal\":\"Work, Employment and Society\",\"abstract\":\"This article evaluates the job quality of work in the remote gig economy. Such work consists of the remote provision of a wide variety of digital services mediated by online labour platforms. Focusing on workers in Southeast Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa, the article draws on semi-structured interviews in six countries ( N = 107) and a cross-regional survey ( N = 679) to detail the manner in which remote gig work is shaped by platform-based algorithmic control. Despite varying country contexts and types of work, we show that algorithmic control is central to the operation of online labour platforms. Algorithmic management techniques tend to offer workers high levels of flexibility, autonomy, task variety and complexity. However, these mechanisms of control can also result in low pay, social isolation, working unsocial and irregular hours, overwork, sleep deprivation and exhaustion.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/0950017018785616\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0950017018785616\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1753},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.4375387\",\"title\":\"Myth of Sharing Economy in Indonesia Gig Work Platform\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Anindya Dessi\"}],\"year\":2023,\"abstract\":\"Mainstream arguments refer to gig work platforms such as ride-hailing, online taxis, and home-sharing as sharing economy platforms. However, this designation is philosophically just a paradox because there is no “true sharing economy” in the gig work platform business model. This paper elaborate the myth of sharing economy by using case studies on food delivery, transportation and goods delivery platforms in Indonesia. The finding shows that those platform companies are often referred to as implementing the sharing economy model, but in reality, the sharing economy concept is not really implemented, because the process of work regulation and distribution of economic results is controlled unilaterally by the platform company. The sharing economy model is not artificially created because it only takes some of the characteristics of the sharing economy and combines it with other economic models so that the distribution is only on risks and costs, but not on income.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.4375387\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4375387\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781800883512.00014\",\"title\":\"Platform work and precariousness: Low earnings and limited control of work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Iain Campbell\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"A Research Agenda for the Gig Economy and Society\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781800883512.00014\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781800883512.00014\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":11},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00015\",\"title\":\"Dual value production as key to the gig economy puzzle\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Niels van Doorn\"},{\"name\":\"Adam Badger\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00015\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00015\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":8},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00014\",\"title\":\"Platform urbanism and infrastructural surplus\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Aaron Shapiro\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00014\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00014\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":3},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.5168285\",\"title\":\"Understanding Gig Economy Labor Supply: Evidence from a Logistics Platform\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Sixing Hu\"},{\"name\":\"Yifan Feng\"},{\"name\":\"Jussi Keppo\"}],\"year\":2025,\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.5168285\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5168285\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-1\",\"title\":\"The Creeping Uberization of Work\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-1\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-1\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5771/9781538173749-135\",\"title\":\"Chapter 8 ﻿Toward Convivial Platform Labor﻿\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Tim Christiaens\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5771/9781538173749-135\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5771/9781538173749-135\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.63090/jeir/3107.9482.0016\",\"title\":\"The Gig Economy and Labor Market Restructuring: Platform Work, Worker Classification, and the Future of Employment Relations\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"V. Basil Han\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"Journal of Economic Insights and Research (JEIR)\",\"abstract\":\"This study examines the growth, characteristics, and labor market implications of platform mediated gig work across 24 OECD countries from 2015 to 2025. Using administrative data, labor force surveys, and platform transaction records, we document that gig work has grown to represent 4.2% of total employment and 12.8% of labor income for participants. We find substantial heterogeneity in worker experiences: approximately 35% of gig workers use platforms as primary income sources with limited alternative opportunities, while 65% engage in platform work as supplementary income alongside traditional employment or education. Earnings analysis reveals that median hourly compensation after accounting for expenses and unpaid time averages $14.20, approximately 22% below comparable traditional employment, though top decile earners achieve premium wages. Worker classification reforms significantly affect platform labor markets: jurisdictions implementing employee classification requirements experience 18% reductions in platform labor supply but 31% increases in hourly compensation for remaining workers. Social protection gaps remain substantial, with only 23% of gig workers reporting access to employer provided health insurance and 12% participating in retirement savings programs. The findings suggest that gig economy expansion reflects both genuine labor market innovation enabling flexibility and cost shifting from firms to workers that policy intervention may appropriately address.\",\"doi\":\"10.63090/jeir/3107.9482.0016\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.63090/jeir/3107.9482.0016\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.3390/soc11030086\",\"title\":\"Constitutional Values in the Gig-Economy? Why Labor Law Fails at Platform Work, and What Can We Do about It?\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Zsolt Ződi\"},{\"name\":\"Bernát Török\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Societies\",\"abstract\":\"Gig-work, or platform work, has been in the crosshairs of regulators since roughly the mid-2010s. The employment of an increasing number of platform workers raises a number of problems, however, there is no longer a consensus as to whether these problems are only the emergence of certain well-established labor law issues in a new guise, or completely new ones. To date, only one possible solution seems to have emerged, that of bringing platform work under the umbrella of labor law. This study argues, on the one hand, that platform work has a characteristic that was previously unknown in the world of labor relations (algorithmic and data-based work organization) and, on the other hand, that it has two other characteristics (tripartite structure and network effect) that create an entirely new quality that requires innovative legal approaches. The study selects some of the recent European Union standards regulating various kinds of online platforms which may also provide useful solutions for the regulation of platform work.\",\"doi\":\"10.3390/soc11030086\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.3390/soc11030086\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":7},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"title\":\"﻿Chapter 4 Alienation ﻿in﻿ ﻿the﻿ Platform Economy﻿\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Tim Christiaens\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0}],\"providersUsed\":[\"openalex\",\"crossref\"],\"providerFailures\":[\"openalex: Literature provider \\\"openalex\\\" is unavailable: rate_limited(429). 其他来源仍可使用；本次未返回伪结果。\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"082c4e83-1b97-424c-854e-536a4d6f22bf"}},"failed":false},{"name":"glob","args":"{\"pattern\":\"review-draft.md\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":1,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_416906dad0c34ff492eb01e7"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_416906dad0c34ff492eb01e7","content":[{"type":"text","text":"review-draft.md"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"9fc9ab62-ece7-4ef2-b942-c58c0bb05af5"},"meta":{"shape":"paths","paths":["review-draft.md"],"truncated":false,"total":1}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":15,\"providers\":\"crossref\",\"query\":\"algorithms at work contested terrain of control Kellogg Valentine Christensen\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_9dfae16ec56341c8bfb4e6e1"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_9dfae16ec56341c8bfb4e6e1","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":15,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"title\":\"Algorithms at Work: The New Contested Terrain of Control\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Katherine C. Kellogg\"},{\"name\":\"Melissa A. Valentine\"},{\"name\":\"Angéle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Annals\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2178},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.001.0001\",\"title\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angele Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"abstract\":\"When the news moved online, journalists suddenly learned what their audiences actually liked, through algorithmic technologies that scrutinize web traffic and activity. Has this advent of audience metrics changed journalists' work practices and professional identities? This book documents the ways that journalists grapple with audience data in the form of clicks, and analyzes how new forms of clickbait journalism travel across national borders. Drawing on four years of fieldwork in web newsrooms in the United States and France, including more than one hundred interviews with journalists, the book reveals many similarities among the media groups examined—their editorial goals, technological tools, and even office furniture. Yet the book uncovers crucial and paradoxical differences in how American and French journalists understand audience analytics and how these affect the news produced in each country. American journalists routinely disregard traffic numbers and primarily rely on the opinion of their peers to define journalistic quality. Meanwhile, French journalists fixate on internet traffic and view these numbers as a sign of their resonance in the public sphere. The book offers cultural and historical explanations for these disparities, arguing that distinct journalistic traditions structure how journalists make sense of digital measurements in the two countries. Contrary to the popular belief that analytics and algorithms are globally homogenizing forces, the book shows that computational technologies can have surprisingly divergent ramifications for work and organizations worldwide.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.001.0001\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.001.0001\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":26},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4135/9781452220727.n12\",\"title\":\"Public Relations as Contested Terrain: A Critical\\n                    Response\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"George Cheney\"},{\"name\":\"Lars ThØger Christensen\"}],\"year\":2001,\"journal\":\"Handbook of Public Relations\",\"doi\":\"10.4135/9781452220727.n12\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4135/9781452220727.n12\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":56},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0008\",\"title\":\"Conclusion\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"abstract\":\"This chapter explores the implications of web analytics for further studies of digital metrics beyond the case of journalism. At a time when nearly every domain is affected by analytics and algorithms, the chapter also provides an overview of what kinds of changes are to be expected and what should not be taken for granted whenever metrics take over. It describes how online media became a different place following the election of Donald Trump as the forty-fifth president of the United States in which news organizations and digital platforms entered into a political and economic maelstrom. It investigates the moral panic surrounding the uncovering of “content farms” and the stream of tweets from the White House labelling mainstream news organizations as “fake news” that caused the media ecosystem to become the center of new controversies about the future of information and democracy. The chapter also shows how news websites can bear some responsibility for problematic developments in journalism.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0008\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0008\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1007/978-1-137-03728-2_1\",\"title\":\"Introduction: anti-racist social work as contested terrain\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Lena Dominelli\"}],\"year\":2008,\"journal\":\"Anti-Racist Social Work\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/978-1-137-03728-2_1\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-137-03728-2_1\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0001\",\"title\":\"Introduction\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"abstract\":\"This chapter examines how the multiplication of digital metrics, analytics, and algorithms is reconfiguring work practices and professional identities. It focuses on the case of journalism, a field that has been profoundly changed by digital technologies. It describes modern newsrooms that use digital tools in the gathering, production, and diffusion of information on the web, from group chats to social media platforms and content management systems. The chapter also introduces a new market that emerged for “web analytics” or software programs that track the behaviour and preferences of internet users. It describes how editors and journalists are provided with a constant stream of data about their audience, receiving increasingly detailed information in real time about the number of visitors, comments, likes, and tweets that their articles attract.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0001\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0001\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.3390/books978-3-7258-4092-2\",\"title\":\"The Contested Terrain of Sport\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2025,\"doi\":\"10.3390/books978-3-7258-4092-2\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.3390/books978-3-7258-4092-2\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0007\",\"title\":\"Between Exposure and Unpaid Work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"abstract\":\"This chapter explores the ramifications of the conflicting systems of evaluation by turning to the thorny question of compensation. It reviews the dilemma of TheNotebook and LaPlace websites on how to get people to work without enough money to pay all contributors a decent wage. The chapter talks about how in both websites traffic became an important criterion for deciding whom to pay and how much to pay them. It examines how compensations and wages affected the identities and careers of freelance journalists in America and France. It also analyzes why American journalists were less conflicted about using metrics-related criteria than their French counterparts.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0007\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0007\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781788978415.00017\",\"title\":\"Contested terrain\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Gerald Epstein\"}],\"year\":2019,\"journal\":\"The Political Economy of Central Banking\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781788978415.00017\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781788978415.00017\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":3},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4018/978-1-60960-587-2.ch706\",\"title\":\"Contested Terrain\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"John Willy Bakke\"}],\"year\":null,\"journal\":\"Global Business\",\"abstract\":\"Workplaces are key loci for expressing and studying organizational identity, even in distributed work. In organization studies, there is a growing recognition of the importance of spatial processes, and workplace design has become an instrument for organizational change. This chapter explores organizational identity through a change process where the office layout was redesigned to strengthen organizational identity and increase productivity. The study shows that identity processes get shaped by the material environment and by technologies enabling distributed and mobile work. It also shows that previous events frame the interpretation of current processes. The chapter is based on a qualitative and quantitative study of the national branch of an international oil company.\",\"doi\":\"10.4018/978-1-60960-587-2.ch706\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4018/978-1-60960-587-2.ch706\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5406/illinois/9780252038709.003.0001\",\"title\":\"Journeys through Contested Terrain\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Sally L. Kitch\"}],\"year\":2017,\"journal\":\"University of Illinois Press\",\"abstract\":\"This chapter presents the author's account of her relationship with two Afghan women, Jamila Afghani, founder and director of an Afghan-based educational NGO then called the Noor Educational Center (NEC), and Marzia Basel, the judge and founder of the Afghan Women Judges Association. These women would eventually ask her to write about their personal stories and political perspectives on Afghan women's tumultuous and contested opportunities and responsibilities, a request that she honored as the core and inspiration of this book. She realized that their journey together—more metaphorical than literal but also involving much travel and change of scene—would take them over such contested terrain.\",\"doi\":\"10.5406/illinois/9780252038709.003.0001\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5406/illinois/9780252038709.003.0001\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1353/jowh.2000.0052\",\"title\":\"Gendering Work, (Re)Working Gender: The Contested Terrain of \\\"Women and Work\\\"\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Jenrose Fitzgerald\"}],\"year\":2000,\"journal\":\"Journal of Women's History\",\"doi\":\"10.1353/jowh.2000.0052\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1353/jowh.2000.0052\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc\",\"title\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"doi\":\"10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":8},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5406/illinois/9780252038709.001.0001\",\"title\":\"Contested Terrain\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Sally L. Kitch\"}],\"year\":2014,\"abstract\":\"This book explores the crisis in contemporary Afghan women's lives by focusing on two remarkable Afghan professional women working on behalf of their Afghan sisters. The book's compelling narrative follows the stories of Judge Marzia Basel and Jamila Afghani from 2005 through 2013, providing an oft-ignored perspective on the personal and professional lives of Afghanistan's women. Contending with the complex dynamics of a society both undergoing and resisting change, Basel and Afghani speak candidly—and critically—of matters like international intervention and patriarchal Afghan culture, capturing the ways in which immense possibility alternates and vies with utter hopelessness. Strongly rooted in feminist theory and interdisciplinary historical and geopolitical analysis, the book sheds new light on the struggle against the powerful forces that affect Afghan women's education, health, political participation, livelihoods, and quality of life. It also suggests how a new dialogue might be started in which women from across geopolitical boundaries might find common cause for change and rewrite their collective stories.\",\"doi\":\"10.5406/illinois/9780252038709.001.0001\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5406/illinois/9780252038709.001.0001\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":3},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc.15\",\"title\":\"BIBLIOGRAPHY\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"doi\":\"10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc.15\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc.15\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"d1c5f715-1d01-4199-afc9-7a0db69ed653"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":15,\"providers\":\"crossref\",\"query\":\"Gandini labour process alienation online gig economy platform\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_277257106a2749099bdd1f25"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_277257106a2749099bdd1f25","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":15,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1177/0018726718790002\",\"title\":\"Labour process theory and the gig economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Alessandro Gandini\"}],\"year\":2018,\"journal\":\"Human Relations\",\"abstract\":\"What are the distinctive traits that characterize work(ing) through (and for) a digital platform? In the burgeoning debate on the ‘gig economy’, a critical examination that comprehensively addresses this issue beyond specific examples or case studies is currently missing. This article uses labour process theory – an important Marxist approach in the study of relations of production in industrial capitalism – to address this gap. Supported by empirical illustrations from existing research, the article discusses the notions of ‘point of production’, emotional labour and control in the gig economy to argue that labour process theory offers a unique set of tools to expand our understanding of the way in which labour power comes to be transformed into a commodity in a context where the encounter between supply and demand of work is mediated by a digital platform, and where feedback, ranking and rating systems serve purposes of managerialization and monitoring of workers.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/0018726718790002\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0018726718790002\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":706},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.3431728\",\"title\":\"Digital Economy and Digital Labour Terminology: Making Sense of the 'Gig Economy', 'Online Labour', 'Crowd Work', 'Microwork', 'Platform Labour', Etc.\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Richard Heeks\"}],\"year\":2017,\"journal\":\"SSRN Electronic Journal\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.3431728\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3431728\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":16},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"title\":\"Alienation in the Platform Economy\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.6543862\",\"title\":\"GIG ECONOMY WORKERS: PLATFORM LABOUR IN INDIA\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Shambhavi Sharma\"}],\"year\":2026,\"abstract\":\"The gig economy has emerged as a significant component of India's labour market, driven by digital platforms that mediate short-term, task-based work across multiple sectors. This article examines the gig economy in the Indian context by analysing the socioeconomic profile of gig workers and the operational dynamics of platform-based labour. It further undertakes a comparative analysis of gig economy frameworks in India and selected foreign jurisdictions to identify regulatory divergences and best practices. The study critically evaluates India's evolving legal framework governing gig work, with particular reference to the Code on Wages, 2019 and the Code on Social Security, 2020, alongside relevant judicial precedents. By incorporating real-life situations, the article highlights the lived realities of gig workers, including algorithmic management, precarious earnings, and limited enforcement of welfare provisions. The article concludes by suggesting a way forward that emphasises regulatory clarity, effective implementation of social security measures, and a balanced approach that reconciles platform innovation with labour rights. This study contributes to contemporary discourse on labour regulation and social justice in the digital economy.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.6543862\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6543862\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4324/9781003161875-19\",\"title\":\"Labour movements, gig economy, and platform capitalism\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Riccardo Emilio Chesta\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"The Routledge Handbook of the Gig Economy\",\"doi\":\"10.4324/9781003161875-19\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003161875-19\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00016\",\"title\":\"Online labour platforms, human resource management and platform ecosystem tensions: an institutional perspective\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Anne Keegan\"},{\"name\":\"Jeroen Meijerink\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00016\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00016\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.6838578\",\"title\":\"Resolving Labour Disputes in the Gig Economy: Can Online Dispute&amp;nbsp;Resolution Bridge the Gap Between Platform Workers and Traditional&amp;nbsp;Labour Justice?\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Hifza Qamar\"},{\"name\":\"Naazish Fatima\"}],\"year\":2026,\"abstract\":\"&lt;span&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The rapid growth of the gig economy has transformed the nature of employment relationships, creating new forms of work that often fall outside the scope of traditional labour protections and dispute resolution mechanisms. Platform-based workers associated with ride-hailing, food delivery, freelance and digital service platforms frequently face issues relating to unfair termination, non-payment of wages, algorithmic penalties, lack of social security, opaque rating systems and arbitrary deactivation of accounts. However, existing labour dispute resolution frameworks in India remain largely designed for conventional employer-employee relationships, leaving gig and platform workers with limited access to timely, affordable and effective remedies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;This paper examines whether Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) can emerge as a practical and accessible mechanism for resolving labour disputes in the gig economy. It analyses the suitability of conciliation, mediation, arbitration and hybrid ADR models in addressing the unique concerns of platform workers, particularly where disputes arise across jurisdictions, involve high-volume low-value claims or require urgent resolution. The paper further studies the limitations of existing labour adjudication under the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947, the Code on Social Security, 2020 and the emerging labour codes in addressing platform-based disputes.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The paper adopts a comparative approach by examining ODR frameworks and gig worker protections in jurisdictions such as the United Kingdom, the European Union and California. Particular attention is paid to recent judicial developments concerning worker classification, platform accountability and digital labour rights. It also explores the role of artificial intelligence, automated decision-making and algorithmic management in creating workplace disputes, while assessing whether ODR mechanisms can provide procedural flexibility without compromising due process, transparency or fairness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The paper argues that India requires a specialised ODR framework for gig and platform workers that combines mediation, digital conciliation and limited appellate review to ensure quick and affordable justice. It proposes the establishment of sector-specific labour ODR platforms, mandatory pre-litigation mediation for platform disputes, standardised grievance redressal systems and statutory safeguards against biased algorithmic decisions. By situating gig work within the broader future of labour regulation, this paper seeks to contribute to the evolving discourse on ADR, labour rights and digital justice in India.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.6838578\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6838578\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_3\",\"title\":\"Digital Caporalato and Migrant Labour in the Gig Economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Gianmarco Peterlongo\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Dynamics of Virtual Work\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_3\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_3\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_2\",\"title\":\"Migrant Labour in the Greek Gig Economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Lefteris Kretsos\"},{\"name\":\"Vassilis Charitsis\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Dynamics of Virtual Work\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_2\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_2\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"title\":\"﻿Chapter 4 Alienation ﻿in﻿ ﻿the﻿ Platform Economy﻿\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Tim Christiaens\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.3280/sl2023-167006\",\"title\":\"Unpacking informality in the gig-economy: ethnographic insights into platform capitalism and its baroque labour process\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Gianmarco Peterlongo\"}],\"year\":2024,\"journal\":\"SOCIOLOGIA DEL LAVORO\",\"doi\":\"10.3280/sl2023-167006\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.3280/sl2023-167006\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":8},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_5\",\"title\":\"Migrant Gig Workers of India’s Consumerist Cities: The Contradictions of Platform Economy and Delivery Work in Neoliberal India\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Suddhabrata Deb Roy\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Dynamics of Virtual Work\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_5\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_5\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.4337/9781839100284.00012\",\"title\":\"Labour protection for non-employees: how the gig economy revives old problems and challenges existing solutions\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Victoria Daskalova\"},{\"name\":\"Shae McCrystal\"},{\"name\":\"Masako Wakui\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Platform Economy Puzzles\",\"doi\":\"10.4337/9781839100284.00012\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.4337/9781839100284.00012\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":7},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_1\",\"title\":\"Introduction: Migrant Labour in the Platform Economy—The Intersection of Labour Digitization, Platform Capitalism, Migration, and Resistance\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Francesco Della Puppa\"},{\"name\":\"Dipsita Dhar\"},{\"name\":\"Nicola Montagna\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Dynamics of Virtual Work\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_1\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91262-7_1\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.3357804\",\"title\":\"Platform Labour and Structured Antagonism: Understanding the Origins of Protest in the Gig Economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Alex Wood\"},{\"name\":\"Vili Lehdonvirta\"}],\"year\":2019,\"abstract\":\"This article investigates why gig economy workers who see themselves as self-employed freelancers also engage in collective action traditionally associated with regular employment. Using ethnographic evidence from remote gig economy workers in North America, the United Kingdom and the Philippines, we argue that labour platforms reduce the risk of false self-employment in terms of the worker-client relationship. However, in doing so, they create new forms of worker dependency on the platforms themselves. We term this relationship ‘platform labour’, and demonstrate that it entails a ‘structured antagonism’ which manifests as perceived conflicts over platform fees, pay rates, and lack of worker voice. This creates desires for representation, greater voice and even unionisation towards the platform, while retaining entrepreneurial attitudes towards clients. By refocusing industrial relations on structured antagonism instead of the employment relationship we can understand conflict, protest and organising in new and diverse forms of work.&lt;br&gt;\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.3357804\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3357804\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":34}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"bcc8d49f-e800-48af-9ff5-ac5076498071"}},"failed":false},{"name":"read","args":"{\"file_path\":\"review-draft.md\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_39569e991eed4a4797117e0a"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_39569e991eed4a4797117e0a","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<path>C:\\Users\\lauze\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\metis-real-agent-Ack7rz\\workspace\\review-draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # 审读报告（真实 Agent E2E 目标文件）\n\n(End of file - total 1 lines)\n</content>"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"fcd4fed1-da6b-46fe-b7cc-b3453f6b7a32"},"meta":{"path":"C:\\Users\\lauze\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\metis-real-agent-Ack7rz\\workspace\\review-draft.md","offset":1,"lines":[{"number":1,"text":"# 审读报告（真实 Agent E2E 目标文件）"}],"totalLines":1,"lang":"md"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_save","args":"{\"records\":\"[{\\\"abstract\\\": \\\"What are the distinctive traits that characterize work(ing) through (and for) a digital platform? In the burgeoning debate on the 'gig economy', a critical examination that comprehensively addresses this issue beyond specific examples or case studies is currently missing. This article uses labour process theory – an important Marxist approach in the study of relations of production in industrial capitalism – to address this gap. Supported by empirical illustrations from existing research, the article discusses the notions of 'point of production', emotional labour and control in the gig economy to argue that labour process theory offers a unique set of tools to expand our understanding of the way in which labour power comes to be transformed into a commodity in a context where the encounter between supply and demand of work is mediated by a digital platform, and where feedback, ranking and rating systems serve purposes of managerialization and monitoring of workers.\\\", \\\"authors\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Alessandro Gandini\\\"}], \\\"citationCount\\\": 706, \\\"doi\\\": \\\"10.1177/0018726718790002\\\", \\\"id\\\": \\\"crossref:10.1177/0018726718790002\\\", \\\"journal\\\": \\\"Human Relations\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"crossref\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Labour process theory and the gig economy\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0018726718790002\\\", \\\"year\\\": 2018}, {\\\"abstract\\\": \\\"This article evaluates the job quality of work in the remote gig economy. Such work consists of the remote provision of a wide variety of digital services mediated by online labour platforms. Focusing on workers in Southeast Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa, the article draws on semi-structured interviews in six countries (N = 107) and a cross-regional survey (N = 679) to detail the manner in which remote gig work is shaped by platform-based algorithmic control. Despite varying country contexts and types of work, we show that algorithmic control is central to the operation of online labour platforms.